  "text": "เคลื่อนไหว\n\nindows[](1,2),(2,4), $(3,3)$ are the vertices of an ascending triangle and passes through the origin. Thus, the circumcircle for this triangle is $x(y-4)+y(x-2)=0$. The line $y=mx+b$, therefore has $m=\\frac 12$ and $b=0$. Thus, our line passes through $(2+\\sqrt 5, 4+\\sqrt 5)$. On the triangle, we see that $(x-1)^2+(y-3)^2=5$. Upon squaring, we see that $(x-1)^2+y^2=20$. Clearly, this only works if $x=3$ and $y=2+\\sqrt 5$. This is not sufficient, because it does not give us a second intersection point. We must have our second intersection point on the second side of the triangle. So we seek two solutions to $(x-2)^2+(y-3)^2=5$ plus two solutions to $(x-1)^2+(y-4)^2=20$. Adding them yields that $x=-4$ and $y=5+\\sqrt 5$ or $x=2+\\sqrt 5$ and $y=5$. Clearly, only the first point can give us the correct length, so our slope is $-\\frac 8{3+\\sqrt 5}=-8-4\\sqrt 5=\\boxed{2-2\\sqrt 5}$.",
